feat: activity tables, and unblock the notification queue

Tasks 2, 3, 4 and 8 of the activity-reminders spec. The schema half, plus
the sender, which came forward because task 4's parity test demands it.

Three tables. The activity/schedule split is load-bearing: there are two
schedule shapes — a wall-clock time on weekdays, and an interval inside a
daily window — and hanging both shapes' columns off `activities` would
leave half of them null on every row and make the CHECK constraint
unreadable. Separate, each shape is required-or-forbidden in one
expression. It also means one activity can own several schedules, so
"swim Tuesday 07:00 and Saturday 09:00" is one activity.

activity_log is separate because it outlives the schedule: deleting an
activity is a statement about the reminder, not a claim that the swim
never happened. So `on delete set null`, and the label is snapshotted —
joining for it would mean renaming an activity retitles history
retroactively.

Three blockers removed from `notifications`, each deliberately:

- `reminder_has_no_fast` said any kind other than 'reminder' MUST have a
  fast_id. True when written, a closed-world assumption, and it rejected
  every activity notification outright. Replaced with a per-kind
  constraint that states the same intent without assuming the set of
  kinds is finished.
- `notifications_unique_per_fast` is `where fast_id is not null`, so it
  cannot see activity rows. A parallel partial index now covers them,
  which is what lets the scheduler be a plain `on conflict do nothing`
  despite running every 15 minutes with a 20-minute lookahead.
- The old dedupe was "one reminder per user per local day", which would
  have collapsed four activities a day into one notification. The index
  replaces it.

Task 4 is the test I most wanted: notification kinds are written three
times — the SQL CHECK, the shared union, and the Edge Function's own
union, which cannot import the shared package — and nothing checked they
agree. Both drifts fail silently. A kind missing from SQL is an insert
that fails inside a security-definer function, logged where nobody
looks. A kind missing from the Edge Function fell off the end of
`compose`, which had no default, returned undefined, and delivered a
push with no title and no body. The test parses all three; `compose` now
has a `never` guard so a missing branch is a build error.

Also fixes a pre-existing bug found while reading the sender: the
`sent_at` update applied to every claimed row, including the stale ones
it had just marked `skipped` two statements earlier. Those rows claimed
both that they were skipped and that they were sent at a specific time,
which is the single question sent_at exists to answer.

Water gets its own notification copy, because "45 minutes" is nonsense
for a volume, and its own deep link to the timer screen where the water
logger lives.

Verified: db reset clean, check:rls passes across 13 tables, and all 144
existing database tests pass unchanged.
